# Data files are written by Python's text-mode open(), which emits CRLF on
# Windows, while the hosted Linux runner writes LF. Letting git normalize them
# made data/liveness.json and the jsonl ledgers permanently "modified" in the
# working tree, which blocked every merge with the runner and cost a stash
# round-trip that nearly stranded 4 rows of real data.
#
# These are append-only records, not source. Git should store the bytes it is
# given and stop rewriting them.
data/** -text
*.jsonl -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APPEND-ONLY LOGS MERGE BY UNION, because losing a pass is worse than a
# duplicate line.
#
# 2026-09-18: a scheduled run collected a full pass, then died in "Commit the
# journal" with add/add and content conflicts across nine data files because a
# human had pushed during the ~10 minutes it was running. `git pull --rebase`
# stopped at the first conflict, the retry loop pushed again from a half-rebased
# tree, and the step exited non-zero. The runner is ephemeral, so THE ROWS WERE
# DISCARDED - infrastructure breaking standing rule 8 on its own.
#
# Every *.jsonl here is a log where each line is independent, so union is the
# correct merge: keep both sides' lines. It can leave a duplicate line, which is
# a cosmetic problem, against losing an hour of collection, which is permanent.
#
# ⚠️ NOT applied to *.json - those are whole-document state files (watchlist,
# quarantine, liveness) where concatenating two versions produces invalid JSON.
# The workflow resolves those by keeping the run's freshly computed version.
#
# ⚠️ The paper ledgers are hash-chained, so a union merge can fork the chain.
# paper.verify() already reports a concurrent-writer fork as OK rather than as
# tampering, and both ledgers are quarantined against writes anyway.
*.jsonl merge=union
